WEBVTT

00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:37.451
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Thursday, July 30th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation.

00:00:38.451 --> 00:00:51.619
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're looking at who controls the tools shaping how Indigenous languages and cultural knowledge get recorded, stored, and shared — and whether that control is actually shifting, or just being talked about differently.

00:00:51.719 --> 00:01:15.327
<v Casey>Quick weather check — Horsefly Lake is sitting at 11 degrees with overcast skies this morning, high of 24 later, and a light drizzle expected tomorrow. Across the region: 100 Mile House at 12, Williams Lake at 13, Quesnel at 11, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Hanceville's holding at 13 — all overcast for now, no driving alerts beyond the usual wildfire smoke advisories.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.148
<v Riley>Let's get into today's stories.

00:01:18.548 --> 00:01:43.068
<v Riley>It's a leaner story count today, but there's a genuine thread running through the on-theme piece, so let's follow it before we move on. Out of India's eastern state of Odisha comes a story about Raja, a rain festival that's supposed to mark the arrival of the monsoon — except this year the monsoon came so late that farmers sowed their seeds under clear skies, waiting for rain that simply wasn't there yet.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.336
<v Casey>A festival built around timing a season that no longer keeps time.

00:01:48.806 --> 00:02:25.158
<v Riley>Exactly, and the piece ties the delay to a "super" El Niño pattern disrupting a rhythm farmers there have organized their entire agricultural calendar around for generations. That calendar isn't superstition — it's generations of observed land knowledge, encoded into ritual because that was the reliable way to pass down a forecasting system before satellites and weather models existed. When the climate stops matching the pattern the knowledge was built on, a community loses more than one harvest. It loses the thing that made the knowledge trustworthy to the next generation in the first place.

00:02:26.488 --> 00:02:44.648
<v Casey>Which is exactly the problem showing up everywhere Indigenous land knowledge meets a changing climate. The land stops confirming what the traditional knowledge predicted, and outside institutions tend to treat that mismatch as proof the knowledge was never rigorous — instead of proof that the climate itself is the thing that moved.

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:59.440
<v Riley>That's basically the frame for today's Deep Dive, actually — who gets to hold knowledge, and on what terms, when the ground underneath it shifts faster than the knowledge can adapt. We'll come back to that once we're through the roundup.

00:03:00.724 --> 00:03:14.620
<v Casey>For now, closer to home. Premier David Eby is rejecting calls from the B.C. Conservatives to lift the province's ban on U.S. alcohol on store shelves, calling it "the only leverage" B.C. still holds in the ongoing trade dispute over softwood lumber tariffs.

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:19.343
<v Riley>Leverage that costs the province liquor sales revenue every month it stays in place.

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:49.707
<v Casey>That's Eby's calculation — that forestry losses under tariffs outweigh what the province gives up on bourbon and bulk wine sales. Eby specifically framed it as protecting "forest families," arguing the Conservatives had met quietly with the U.S. ambassador and come back arguing for exactly the opposite position. Speaking of the B.C. Conservatives, the party is also without an executive director this week — Angelo Isidorou has departed the role, with no successor named yet and no public explanation given for the timing.

00:03:51.012 --> 00:04:11.180
<v Riley>And a quick driving note for anyone moving through the region — Drive BC has confirmed Highway 97 South at Kozuki Road, just south of Williams Lake, has reopened in both directions following a motor vehicle incident. Worth checking DriveBC before any Cariboo road trip this week regardless, given everything else going on with wildfires.

00:04:12.625 --> 00:04:37.185
<v Casey>Which brings us to the wildfire cluster, and there's a real one this week. The Bowers Lake fire, burning between Clearwater and 100 Mile House, held steady at roughly 150 hectares as of Monday — not expanding dramatically, but not out either. Further south, several fires near Clinton have merged and grown large enough to generate their own weather system, with lightning and erratic wind patterns making suppression harder.

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:43.633
<v Riley>That's a scale of fire behaviour that used to be rare in this region and increasingly isn't.

00:04:44.833 --> 00:05:04.129
<v Casey>Residents of 70 Mile and the surrounding area were told to prepare for a possible Monday morning evacuation order as a direct result. Clearwater's mayor has also gone public reminding residents to have a wildfire go-bag ready and know their evacuation route in advance — a message that applies well beyond Clearwater's town limits this season.

00:05:05.455 --> 00:05:27.703
<v Riley>Not a bad idea for anyone in the Cariboo right now, frankly, given how quickly some of these fires have been shifting behaviour. On the response side, crews from Yukon Protective Services, Alberta Wildfire, and teams from Mexico are being brought in over the coming days to reinforce B.C.'s wildfire response capacity, according to a B.C. Wildfire Service situation report.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:39.492
<v Casey>That's the kind of interprovincial and international mutual-aid arrangement that's become almost routine in B.C. summers now, which says something about where fire seasons are trending.

00:05:40.695 --> 00:05:49.735
<v Riley>On a lighter note, Williams Lake's cathedral bells are marking ninety years of continuous ringing, still functioning mechanically after nearly a century of use.

00:05:49.635 --> 00:05:53.307
<v Casey>Ha. Some infrastructure just needs bell-ringers, not a software patch.

00:05:54.526 --> 00:06:10.294
<v Riley>And an update worth flagging with real relief — the man previously reported missing near Kelowna, believed to be walking toward Anahim Lake, has since been found safe. Good news on a story that could easily have gone the other way given the distance and terrain involved.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:47.274
<v Casey>Also worth noting today — there's an active question about Facebook accounts tied to alleged foreign interference in B.C. politics, with investigators still working through the scope of who ran them and what they targeted. We don't have enough confirmed detail yet to go deeper than that, but it's one to watch as more comes out. And the Quesnel Hospital Auxiliary has put out a call for new volunteers to keep supporting patients at the hospital — always worth an email to them directly if you've got a few hours a month to spare, especially in a community that size where volunteer capacity really does determine what services stay running.

00:06:48.666 --> 00:07:06.234
<v Riley>And one more before we move on — the Williams Lake and District 4-H Show and Sale is confirmed to go ahead for its 68th year, a reminder that some of the Cariboo's longest-running community institutions are still finding a way to keep operating season after season, wildfire smoke and all.

00:07:06.907 --> 00:07:10.659
<v Casey>Sixty-eight years running is its own kind of infrastructure resilience.

00:07:11.342 --> 00:07:15.438
<v Riley>That's the roundup for today. Let's head into the Community Spotlight.

00:07:16.838 --> 00:07:51.238
<v Riley>Today we're spotlighting T'exelcemc, the Williams Lake Indian Band — a self-governing First Nation running programs across language revitalization, housing, and land stewardship right here in the Cariboo. Their language work is part of a broader effort across Secwépemc communities to keep Secwepemctsín spoken by new generations, not just archived for outside researchers to study. It's the kind of program that only works when a community controls the pace, the priorities, and the people involved from the start. You can learn more about their programs at wlfn.ca.

00:07:51.781 --> 00:08:04.381
<v Casey>Governance and language work sitting under the same roof isn't an accident — it's usually the Nations doing both at once that make the language work actually stick over the long run, rather than fading after the first grant cycle ends.

00:08:04.481 --> 00:08:07.441
<v Casey>Which is a pretty good hinge into where we're headed next.

00:08:08.841 --> 00:08:33.721
<v Riley>A couple of weeks back we got deep into data sovereignty broadly — exclusion versus inclusion, who controls interpretation once a Nation's data leaves the building. Today the question is narrower, and honestly more concrete: when it comes specifically to language and cultural revitalization — not land surveys, not health records — are the tools actually built for Indigenous control, or just marketed that way?

00:08:33.999 --> 00:08:44.015
<v Casey>Narrower, but not smaller. Language is the thing everybody in this space claims to care about, which makes it also the thing every platform wants to attach itself to for credibility.

00:08:44.666 --> 00:09:16.050
<v Riley>There's real reason for optimism here, though, and it's worth starting with the strongest case. Wikimedia Australia just released an Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property and Data Sovereignty guide, developed with Dr. Terri Janke and her team, specifically asking editors to check who has the cultural authority to tell a given story before they write the Wikipedia article about it. That's a governance question built directly into an editing workflow — not a values statement bolted onto a press release after the fact.

00:09:16.585 --> 00:09:25.097
<v Casey>A guide is a document, though. What happens when an editor on the other side of the world ignores it and posts the article anyway, five minutes after it's published?

00:09:25.434 --> 00:09:43.426
<v Riley>The guide doesn't claim to solve enforcement on its own — it's explicit that it's a starting framework, not a lockout mechanism. But it's a genuinely different design choice than what usually happens on open platforms, which is publish first and correct later, if someone complains loudly enough to get noticed.

00:09:44.113 --> 00:10:02.281
<v Casey>Which is still the same failure mode we ran into with OCAP a couple of weeks back — the framework exists, published and public, but nothing technically stops a well-meaning outside contributor from moving faster than the consultation process it describes. Good intentions don't function as an access control. They never have.

00:10:02.855 --> 00:10:37.079
<v Riley>Fair — and that's actually why the BetaKit piece on the Mila Indigenous AI Gathering feels like a genuinely different case, worth separating out from the Wikimedia example. That gathering wasn't outside institutions asking Indigenous communities to comment on a tool they'd already built and shipped. It was Indigenous technologists — people like Ryan St. Germaine of Indigenous Tech Circle — in the room from the design stage, talking about how to build AI without data extraction as the baseline requirement going in, not an ethics add-on stapled on at the end.

00:10:37.604 --> 00:10:51.500
<v Casey>Building AI without data extraction is the stated goal. What's the actual mechanism, though? Who owns the model weights if a language-learning tool ends up trained on recordings of fluent Elders speaking a language with only a few dozen remaining speakers?

00:10:51.822 --> 00:11:29.566
<v Riley>That's exactly where the CARE Principles come in — Collective Benefit, Authority to Control, Responsibility, and Ethics. They were developed specifically to sit on top of the FAIR open-data standards and say, in effect, that technical openness alone doesn't automatically serve the people the data came from. Pair CARE with OCAP — Ownership, Control, Access, Possession — which the First Nations Information Governance Centre has been operationalizing in Canada for close to three decades now, and you've got something more solid than a stated value. You've got a framework with actual institutional precedent behind it.

00:11:30.079 --> 00:12:07.047
<v Casey>OCAP has existed since the nineteen-nineties. Nearly thirty years, and it still hasn't stopped extractive land and data deals from moving forward around Nations that have every legal right to say no under that exact framework. Look at what's happening right now near Reno, Nevada — an industrial park with several planned data centers threatens the water supply feeding Pyramid Lake, land and water central to the Pyramid Lake Paiute Tribe's homeland. Construction jobs show up for a year or two during buildout, then largely evaporate once the facility goes operational, but the water draw doesn't go away. It's permanent infrastructure built on a temporary jobs promise.

00:12:07.547 --> 00:12:24.507
<v Riley>That's a fundamentally different category of technology, though. That's hyperscale physical infrastructure sited directly on contested land and water rights. We're talking here about language tools built collaboratively with communities, not data centers built next door to them without consent.

00:12:25.043 --> 00:13:02.211
<v Casey>Except they're not as unrelated as that framing suggests. The exact reporting Grist and the Indigenous News Alliance did on data centers threatening Indigenous land is the same AI boom that's now funding the "Indigenous AI" gatherings and the language-model pilots getting celebrated in the same news cycle. The capital behind Mila's investment panel — Raven Indigenous Capital Partners, BDC Capital — is downstream of the exact same investment flows building gigawatt-scale data centers on tribal water in Nevada and across Alberta's boreal forest. Enthusiasm for one side of that industry doesn't cancel out scrutiny of the other side. If anything, it should sharpen it.

00:13:02.061 --> 00:13:04.757
<v Riley>That's a real tension. I'll give you that one directly.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:23.368
<v Casey>The actual question a Nation building a language app has to ask isn't just "does this respect CARE principles" in the abstract. It's "whose server is this actually running on, and what happens to my recordings of a ninety-year-old fluent speaker if that company gets acquired in five years by someone who never signed onto CARE at all."

00:13:24.016 --> 00:14:03.552
<v Riley>Which is exactly the kind of design decision the FirstVoices platform got right early, and it's worth being specific about why. FirstVoices was purpose-built from the start for Indigenous language archiving with governance controls baked in — not a general-purpose AI tool retrofitted afterward for language work because someone realized there was a grant available. Communities decide what gets published, what stays restricted to community members only, who counts as an authorized speaker for a given word or story. That's a template that's already been running successfully for years across dozens of Nations, not a promise made in a pitch deck.

00:14:04.067 --> 00:14:44.067
<v Casey>FirstVoices works precisely because it was designed around restriction as a feature, not a bug to be minimized. Most commercial AI language tools are optimizing for the opposite instinct — maximum data ingestion, maximum reuse across products, because that's what makes a model more capable and a company more valuable. A voice model trained to help teach Secwepemctsín pronunciation needs recordings of fluent speakers to work at all. Once those recordings exist digitally on a commercial platform, that platform holds leverage the Nation may not have explicitly signed away — but also didn't necessarily retain control over, depending on how carefully anyone read the terms of service before uploading.

00:14:44.668 --> 00:15:21.636
<v Riley>So the real design question for a program like T'exelcemc's language work isn't whether to use a modern recording or transcription tool at all — it's which one, and under what terms of data custody specifically. A cloud-based transcription service from a company whose terms include a clause allowing them to "use your content to improve our models" is categorically different from something built on a platform like FirstVoices, or hosted entirely on Nation-controlled servers, even if the surface-level feature — helping a learner hear correct pronunciation — looks functionally identical to a user.

00:15:22.185 --> 00:15:41.201
<v Casey>And that decision has to get made before the recordings exist, not retroactively after the fact. Once an Elder's voice is sitting inside a commercial company's training pipeline, walking that back is a legal fight requiring lawyers and leverage most small Nations don't have sitting around, not a settings toggle you flip off in an account menu.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:16:06.756
<v Riley>Williams Lake Indian Band's language program is actually a good real-world test case for exactly this, given they're running revitalization work alongside housing and land stewardship, all under their own governance structure. If they're weighing tools for language documentation right now, the CARE and OCAP frameworks give them something concrete to hold a vendor to contractually — not just something to appeal to morally in a partnership meeting.

00:16:07.383 --> 00:16:38.999
<v Casey>Contractually is the right word there, and it's the whole distinction. A vendor can say all the right things about respecting Indigenous data sovereignty in a press release or a conference panel. What actually matters is whether the signed contract lets the Nation revoke access, pull every recording out, and switch platforms entirely without losing the archive they've spent years building. That's the exact same test we landed on a couple of weeks back with the coproduction question — who can actually say no, later, once the project's already running and everyone's invested in it continuing.

00:16:39.516 --> 00:17:11.100
<v Riley>Where I'd push back a little is that this generation of tools genuinely does make that "no" more exercisable than it used to be, technically speaking. Open-source speech and language models mean a Nation isn't locked into one company's proprietary file format or cloud account the way they would have been even five years ago. Secwepemctsín recordings can, in principle, be trained into a smaller model that runs on hardware the Nation itself controls, rather than sitting permanently inside a hyperscaler's cloud infrastructure.

00:17:11.786 --> 00:17:39.674
<v Casey>In principle. Running your own model requires actual compute, and compute increasingly means either buying serious hardware outright or renting time from exactly the kind of data center that Pyramid Lake is currently fighting in court and in the streets. There's no clean way to route entirely around that industry right now — the real question is just how much dependency a given Nation is willing to carry, for how long, and what happens if the terms change midway through a multi-year language program.

00:17:40.291 --> 00:18:04.203
<v Riley>That's a legitimate constraint, and I'm not going to pretend it isn't real. But it's a different kind of constraint than "the technology can't be made to respect sovereignty at all." It's closer to "sovereignty currently has real infrastructure costs attached to it" — which is at least a solvable engineering and funding problem, rather than a permanent structural dead end with no path forward.

00:18:04.803 --> 00:18:43.003
<v Casey>Solvable in theory doesn't mean solved in practice, and a lot of "solvable" problems in this space have stayed unsolved for a decade because nobody had the standing capacity to actually do the work of solving them. What would genuinely move this forward for a Cariboo Nation right now isn't waiting around for a perfect open-source stack to materialize — it's running a smaller, deliberately limited experiment first. Something like a single Secwepemctsín word-of-the-week pronunciation tool, hosted and controlled entirely on infrastructure the Nation owns outright, tested quietly with a small handful of learners, before any Elder's full voice archive ever touches a third-party server anywhere.

00:18:43.648 --> 00:18:56.352
<v Riley>That's a fair place to actually land on this — start small, keep custody entirely local from day one, and only scale up once the terms have been proven in practice, not just promised in a proposal document.

00:18:56.979 --> 00:19:17.795
<v Casey>And that's still a meaningfully different approach than the one everyone keeps defaulting to for Nations by habit, which is "adopt the platform now, and worry about the fine print later once it's already in use." Language work specifically is too slow, too irreplaceable, and too tied to a shrinking number of fluent speakers to run on somebody else's commercial product timeline.

00:19:18.495 --> 00:19:43.367
<v Riley>If anyone listening has actually seen a Cariboo language program navigate this in real life — which specific tools they picked, what the data-custody terms in that contract actually looked like — that's genuinely worth an email to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. That's exactly the kind of ground-truth detail that would sharpen this conversation well beyond what we can piece together from public reporting alone.

00:19:43.995 --> 00:19:56.155
<v Casey>Genuinely — send it in if you've got it. We're working from published sources here, and this is one area in particular where the Nations actually doing the work know things we simply don't have access to from the outside.

00:19:56.812 --> 00:20:02.084
<v Riley>That's the show for today. Thanks for spending part of your Thursday with us here in the Cariboo.

00:20:02.721 --> 00:20:08.057
<v Casey>Same time tomorrow. Take care of yourselves out there, and stay clear of the fire zones if you're near one.